course: dictionary
lesson: 1277
title: Busy and its meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word busy has more than one meaning, so context tells you which one to use. «Mum is busy cooking» means she has a lot to do right now. «A busy road» means full of traffic and movement.
explain: Look at the words near busy and ask what it describes: a person, a place or a picture. In «She's busy», Maddox means his mum has too much to do to come to sports day. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Busy can change its form: busier and busiest compare how much is going on. You can also use busy as a verb, as in busy yourself, which means to keep yourself occupied. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: «Busy» means having a lot to do, or working on something now.
fact: «Busy» can mean full of people, traffic or activity, as in «a busy road» or «a busy shop».
fact: On a phone, a busy line means it is in use. In the UK we say engaged.
fact: «Busy» can also describe a picture or pattern with too many details.
fact: Busy roads are dangerous, so cross only at crossings, hold an adult's hand, and stop, look and listen.
exercise: «Mum is busy cooking.» What does busy mean here? | In use, like a phone line | Full of people and traffic | A pattern with too many details | *Having a lot to do right now
exercise: «Duck-billed dinosaurs looked after their babies in busy nesting colonies.» What does busy suggest about the colonies? | Hidden under the ground | Painted in bright colours | *Full of activity | Empty and quiet
exercise: Maddox tells his mates, «She's busy,» because he wants to protect her. Why does he say it? | He thinks sports day is a busy road | He wants his mates to visit her | *He wants to protect his mum from questions about being at sports day | He thinks his mum is cooking dinner
test: «Long before Europeans came, the Indian Ocean was a busy trade world.» What does busy mean here? | Having a lot to do right now | *Full of activity and movement | Quiet and empty | A picture with too many details
test: Which phrase in the lessons means very active, like a bee? | Keep busy | *As busy as a bee | Busybody | Get busy
exam: Which sentence uses busy to mean full of traffic? | «She's busy, so she cannot come.» | *«A busy road is dangerous, so cross only at crossings.» | «The phone line is busy.» | «Mum is busy cooking.»
